#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/99/99eef26d2de27b8ae6f8cf872b352368efaf2703e47bcea6e406478db14d6e18.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7d00c49df06196f76846eeee5152f258a53c4936b51285233531872906ced6b.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2682751-23e4-5cf2-9a46-82c75765423f" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad4ddff-b776-50d9-b698-c0172ee9d3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12328" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D (2x+y)y^2 \\:\\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(\\dfrac32,0\\right) ,$ $\\left(\\dfrac52,0\\right) ,$ $(1,3),$ and $(0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d526a4897f071f523b5900c55fd8797cf5e4270a3031270013e54dc9dca9ca4.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/ae/aeb4cdc398b9f13551ecea013fef9ea607786ab1f9d3466da846dd10b930f378.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/e9/e9a07b75be73621bb23edb6f4da67999c5f5ce07b8776f549cf12464e74fa702.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/a1/a1228e77194b92d8b2c527845273ae9f70d9b90d4c3091f5ed9dd9f8c430776c.svg)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2682751-23e4-5cf2-9a46-82c75765423f" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a70e77-e143-5fe1-987f-6cf3d9622cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480759} {:answer/id #uuid "16926c3a-001d-572a-beb5-ac09cba7435e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480780} {:answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} {:answer/id #uuid "943844b2-6990-576d-9141-b10699c96298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481018} {:answer/id #uuid "96c98676-4b18-52e8-baf0-b525847f1830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930bb660-443d-5920-8d2a-07cfdc6d0a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481014} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dca1b12-338a-5c5f-badf-6213000ef5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d5d128b-3cee-5601-8961-cb121a4bf681" :question/math-academy-id "q-160903" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,1),$ $(3,0),$ and $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081fd6aa-767f-5e6c-8063-fd90ac7a2f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373932} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b43986-8188-5a82-ad1b-7a891ddf5d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373933} {:answer/id #uuid "89664c48-9f18-5fb8-a023-18d5e5ef618f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-{e}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373934} {:answer/id #uuid "0760685b-3486-5566-8fb0-251787b04fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({e}^{2}-{e}^{-2})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373935} {:answer/id #uuid "541abc1d-61aa-5674-93ce-046f9a061a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b43986-8188-5a82-ad1b-7a891ddf5d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373933} :answer-field/id #uuid "83574290-1e94-5b24-a81e-a0a3bedf25ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "077db48c-c6f6-5981-a54c-8cbea8e78815" :question/math-academy-id "q-160911" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2v}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}-u]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494431} {:answer/id #uuid "377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494433} {:answer/id #uuid "389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494436} {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494571} {:answer/id #uuid "adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494571} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160915" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494570} {:answer/id #uuid "807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494600} {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494608} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494739} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494608} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-160917" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2682751-23e4-5cf2-9a46-82c75765423f" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e03497-2478-5699-b8c8-60dc8393efd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480771} {:answer/id #uuid "14aeda53-bc0f-5285-a152-aac2c1de7b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480775} {:answer/id #uuid "31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480835} {:answer/id #uuid "328e9ba1-7166-5fcf-9c18-bf5334f7dc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480837} {:answer/id #uuid "c05027eb-9d67-552f-950c-d7aa5524cbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31768ff6-9f0c-50bd-9796-46c6ac637b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480835} :answer-field/id #uuid "e051d3d7-1ee2-505b-957c-b010b42121b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b878a44e-123c-5f2f-a474-6dc1888b9c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-160938" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e43e2c3-5048-5e2e-a94a-bcb90d586202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-{u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375105} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} {:answer/id #uuid "f152898a-1a3f-57ba-8463-c69b4da0c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-{v}^{2}+3{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375107} {:answer/id #uuid "3b55205c-68ca-554b-93eb-3fc21cfe0e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-2{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375108} {:answer/id #uuid "11837235-5be9-5816-bdf3-58b69896ee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-5{v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} :answer-field/id #uuid "227ed5b3-0cfb-51b9-820b-b40696387d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db1fee9d-f261-54f6-9563-ad9a8f609d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38369" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+y\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+y$ and $v=y,$ the integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{(\\frac{u-v}{2})}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b519e50-b326-56a3-8360-01cf01bef0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375511} {:answer/id #uuid "8717345d-af4e-5f8b-876a-46ab0b5392c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375512} {:answer/id #uuid "de05559f-528d-50d8-9fa6-bf9cc362a558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375513} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4bc111-f102-5feb-91f1-f0edd3cc5425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecb057f-1869-565e-8e7e-c9b2d50df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e4bc111-f102-5feb-91f1-f0edd3cc5425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} :answer-field/id #uuid "da395623-3256-578e-8977-74807708542d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab9f14a-bb15-51f5-bbda-2778b7330c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83647" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
